=== HINT 1: Allocate slot ===
g_tls_slot = TlsAlloc();
if (g_tls_slot == TLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES) { /* error */ }

=== HINT 2: Set value (thread function) ===
TlsSetValue(g_tls_slot, (void*)(uintptr_t)tid);

=== HINT 3: Get value ===
DWORD stored = (DWORD)(uintptr_t)TlsGetValue(g_tls_slot);
assert(stored == tid);

=== HINT 4: Free slot (main, after all threads finish) ===
TlsFree(g_tls_slot);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
TlsGetValue returns the value SET by THE CURRENT THREAD for that slot.
Thread A's TlsSetValue does not affect Thread B's TlsGetValue - they
each have independent storage even though they use the same slot index.

Static alternative (compiler extension, not portable):
  __declspec(thread) DWORD thread_id;   // implicit per-thread storage
Dynamic TLS is needed when you don't know the slot at compile time
(e.g., a DLL that must work with any calling process).
